-3 && git diff d3e2ba8 HEAD"], ["timeout", "270000"]], "output": "```console\n9784367 Outline rev 7: null-predicted k* is not search-free; the search-free predictor is C5\nd3e2ba8 Outline rev 6: Fig. 1 two panels, equal-N is Fig. 2, float counts follow the venue cap\naced37e Outline rev 5: answers review 2 (disclosure in the note's words, abstract rows, lead on counts/null/prediction, calibration plot, compact Table 5 in main)\ndiff --git a/Outline.md b/Outline.md\nindex 4d78a0e..8006bcf 100644\n--- a/Outline.md\n+++ b/Outline.md\n@@ -1,4 +1,4 @@\n-# Outline (revision 6, answers review 2 and figtab's figure decisions)\n+# Outline (revision 7, answers review 2 and figtab's figure decisions)\n \n Works from the `Brief` of `idea-repo` (wiki revision 6; the corrected noise curve and \"Q ≥ 1 only in the linear regime\" are in since its revision 4). The outline says what to test and what each part of the paper must say; it does not say what the result is. Every claim below has the outcome that would make it false and the sentence the paper then writes instead.\n \n@@ -6,7 +6,7 @@ Works from the `Brief` of `idea-repo` (wiki revision 6; the corrected noise curv\n \n Pairwise-comparison leaderboards (Chatbot Arena, MT-Bench, sports ratings) are Bradley–Terry fits whose published uncertainty is a bootstrap CI. A CI answers \"would another sample of the same process reorder the models?\". It does not answer the two questions readers and attackers actually have: *how many of the existing comparisons carry this conclusion*, and *is that number large or small for a dataset of this size and spread?* The raw answer (\"dropping 0.1% of the votes flips the top model\") is expected not to be comparable across datasets, because theory says the number of alterations needed grows with the statistical separation z and with √N; whether this holds on real data is tested in 5.1 and first on synthetic BT data, and the introduction's example is written to match whichever way it comes out. We give a common scale (alterations in standard-error units, compared against a parametric-bootstrap null of the same graph) and apply it to crowd, expert, LLM-judge and sports data, with matched contrasts that separate \"design\" from \"statistics\". The paper reports what the measurements show, including \"fragility is just statistics\", \"it depends on conditions\", or \"unexpected\".\n \n-**What the lead is (decided now, before the full results).** The paper's main sentence is about *counts, the null and the prediction*: how many comparisons carry a conclusion as a function of z, N and graph density (Table 2); how close the same-graph BT null predicts that count, with no search (calibration plot, Fig. 1; C4, C5); how that compares with random noise at equal budget (C2); and how much of the CI-separated view survives (C3). Q = k\\*·g_max/gap and F = Q·z are introduced as the *gauge* that makes datasets comparable and as a check on the search, not as the finding: if Q_real ≈ Q_null ≈ 1.1, \"F collapses on z\" is close to the first-order identity k\\*·g_max ≈ gap, a correct diagnostic that the paper states in a line and does not headline. If the real data leave the null, the gauge is what shows where and by how much, and the lead becomes that departure.\n+**What the lead is (decided now, before the full results).** The paper's main sentence is about *counts, the null and the prediction*: how many comparisons carry a conclusion as a function of z, N and graph density (Table 2); how close the same-graph BT null predicts that count (calibration plot, Fig. 1; C4, C5); how that compares with random noise at equal budget (C2); and how much of the CI-separated view survives (C3). Q = k\\*·g_max/gap and F = Q·z are introduced as the *gauge* that makes datasets comparable and as a check on the search, not as the finding: if Q_real ≈ Q_null ≈ 1.1, \"F collapses on z\" is close to the first-order identity k\\*·g_max ≈ gap, a correct diagnostic that the paper states in a line and does not headline. If the real data leave the null, the gauge is what shows where and by how much, and the le\n[truncated for publication]", "exit": null}}}, {"id": "d2027295-d5a7-5256-a7f0-6197a648d716", "at": 1795669308.5404584, "clock": "host", "stream": "runner", "sequence": 30463, "layer": "worker", "category": "message", "kind": "session.session/request_permission", "role": "idea-review-repo", "actor": "idea-review-repo", "execution_id": "b2dbe9ac8ce59a0f3ee6a06a40cd529a", "trust": "agent-reported", "outcome": null, "label": "message", "repository": null, "flags": [], "causes": [], "summary": "session/request_permission"}, {"id": "d5959a0e-edc5-52e5-9a27-be28444bb45c", "at": 1795669308.542983, "clock": "host", "stream": "runner", "sequence": 30464, "layer": "worker", "category": "message", "kind": "session.response", "role": "idea-review-repo", "actor": "idea-review-repo", "execution_id": "b2dbe9ac8ce59a0f3ee6a06a40cd529a", "trust": "agent-reported", "outcome": null, "label": "message", "repository": null, "flags": [], "causes": [], "summary": "response"}, {"id": "267dacaa-ad13-56f1-9c88-327c1502cb71", "at": 1795669308.5448563, "clock": "host", "stream": "runner", "sequence": 30465, "layer": "worker", "category": "usage", "kind": "session.usage", "role": "idea-review-repo", "actor": "idea-review-repo", "execution_id": "b2dbe9ac8ce59a0f3ee6a06a40cd529a", "trust": "agent-reported", "outcome": null, "label": "usage", "repository": null, "flags": [], "causes": [], "summary": "context usage"}, {"id": "80d2c9da-c579-5ba9-a2d2-a85246f72f53", "at": 1795669308.6640313, "clock": "host", "stream": "runner", "sequence": 30467, "layer": "worker", "category": "challenge", "kind": "authentication.challenge", "role": "idea-review-repo", "actor": "unknown", "execution_id": "b2dbe9ac8ce59a0f3ee6a06a40cd529a", "trust": "system-confirmed", "outcome": "credential-required", "label": "challenge → credential-required", "repository": null, "flags": [], "causes": [], "summary": "crede